+## Roles: exactly one link per pair
+
+Every device both advertises (peripheral role) and scans (central role), so two devices in range
+see each other on both sides. To avoid two parallel links (which used to produce frames of different
+links ending up in one session), the role is decided **before** connecting:
+
+- On every `start()` a device draws a random 4-byte **nonce** and advertises it in the service data
+  (see "Advertising"). It stays the same until the next `start()` (or until a tie, see below).
+- When a scan result with a matching token shows a peer's nonce, the device compares the two nonces
+  as lower-case hex strings: the device with the **smaller nonce initiates** (connects as GATT
+  central), the other one **waits** (stays GATT peripheral, never calls `connect()` for that peer).
+  Both sides evaluate the same two nonces and reach opposite conclusions.
+- **Equal nonces** (practically never): neither side connects; both draw a new nonce and
+  re-advertise it, and the next scan result breaks the tie.
+- The initiating side keeps **at most one connect in flight** (across all peers) and at most one link
+  per remote address; scan results for an address that already has a link are ignored. After a failed
+  connect it backs off per peer: 2 s, 4 s, 8 s ... up to 30 s. After an established link ended it
+  waits 2 s before reconnecting; a link that dies in the handshake counts as a failure.
+- Links are identified by **remote address + GATT role**; each link has its own session and frames
+  are always delivered to the session of the link they arrived on. The native module therefore tags
+  `nearbyFrame`/`nearbyDisconnected`/`nearbyMtu` events with the role and `send()`/`disconnect()`
+  take it as a parameter.
+
+### Link ready conditions
+
+A link is only reported to the session layer (`nearbyConnected`) once frames can flow in both
+directions:
+
+- **Central** (the side that called `connect()`): GATT connected, services discovered (rx and tx
+  present), MTU negotiated (`requestMtu(517)`, falling back to 23), and the CCCD of `tx` written
+  successfully. The whole sequence must finish within 10 s, otherwise the central cancels, closes the
+  GATT object and reports the failure (with the GATT status name, e.g. `133 GATT_ERROR`,
+  `147 CONN_TIMEOUT`, `19 REMOTE_TERMINATED`) to JS. The scan is paused while a connect is running
+  and resumed afterwards.
+- **Peripheral**: the central has enabled notifications on `tx` (a CCCD write with
+  `ENABLE_NOTIFICATION_VALUE`). The MTU from the GATT server's `onMtuChanged` is stored per device
+  and delivered with the connected event; later MTU changes arrive as `nearbyMtu` events and the
+  session adapts its fragment size.
+- Writes to `rx` (and notifications on `tx`) that arrive before the link is ready are buffered by
+  the native module and delivered right after the connected event, in order. The TS layer also
+  buffers frames that arrive before the session of their link exists, and replays them.
+
+### Duplicate-connection resolution (safety net)
+
+If, despite the above (e.g. a peer on an older build that doesn't advertise a nonce), two sessions
+do end up open to the same peer (detected once both sides' `Hello` messages reveal the same peer
+pubkey on two links), the rule is: **the connection whose central side has the numerically smaller
+pubkey (as a hex string) wins**; the other is disconnected.
 
